%[text] # WD Mechanical Data Preprocessing with Live Tasks and Signal Analyzer
%[text] This app-first workshop shows how Mechanical engineers can import measurement data, clean it interactively with Live Editor tasks, and explore the result in MATLAB apps. The emphasis is on an easy workflow that automatically generates reusable MATLAB code.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Workshop flow"}
%[text] - Import a realistic CSV measurement
%[text] - Clean missing samples and outliers with Live Tasks
%[text] - Remove drift and smooth noise with Live Tasks
%[text] - Explore time and frequency content in Signal Analyzer
%[text] - Generate reusable code from the App \
%%
%[text] ## 1. Import a realistic mechanical measurement
%[text] Run this section first. It loads the supplied CSV and creates a training copy with missing samples, impulse outliers, and slow sensor drift. These are common issues in test and failure-analysis data.
workshopFolder = fileparts(which('WD_Mechanical_CSV_App_Workshop'));
data = readtable(fullfile(workshopFolder, 'sample_servo_data.csv'));
time_s = data.Time_s;
accelerationRaw = data.Acceleration_g;
accelerationRaw = accelerationRaw + 0.08*(time_s/time_s(end));
missingIndex = round(linspace(0.18,0.72,4)*height(data));
outlierIndex = round(linspace(0.28,0.82,4)*height(data));
accelerationRaw(missingIndex) = NaN;
accelerationRaw(outlierIndex) = accelerationRaw(outlierIndex) + [1.2;-1.0;1.1;-1.2];
mechanicalData = table(time_s, accelerationRaw, data.PositionError_um, data.MotorCurrent_A, ...
    VariableNames={'Time_s','AccelerationRaw_g','PositionError_um','MotorCurrent_A'});
ax = axes;
plot(ax, time_s, accelerationRaw, LineWidth=1)
grid(ax, 'on')
xlabel(ax, 'Time (s)')
ylabel(ax, 'Acceleration (g)')
title(ax, 'Raw measurement: gaps, spikes, drift, and noise')
%%
%[text] ## 2. Live Task — Clean Missing Data
%[text] ![](text:image:4c26)
%[text] Place the cursor below this paragraph. On the **Live Editor** tab, select **Task \> Clean Missing Data**. Choose `accelerationRaw` as the input, select **Fill missing \> Linear interpolation**, use `time_s` as the x-axis data, and name the output `accelerationFilled`.
%[text] Expand **Show generated code** so the customer can see that the point-and-click task creates readable MATLAB code. The line below is the equivalent fallback, so the workshop can continue even if the task is not inserted.
accelerationFilled = fillmissing(accelerationRaw, 'linear', SamplePoints=time_s);
%%
%[text] ## 3. Live Task — Clean Outlier Data
%[text] Add **Task \> Clean Outlier Data**. Use `accelerationFilled` as the input, select a moving-median detection method, fill detected outliers by linear interpolation, and name the output `accelerationNoOutliers`. Adjust the window while watching the task preview.
%[text] The generated-code equivalent below uses a 101-sample moving window. This step comes before detrending and smoothing so impulse spikes do not bias or spread into later calculations.
[accelerationNoOutliers, outlierLocations] = filloutliers(accelerationFilled, 'linear', 'movmedian', 101, SamplePoints=time_s);
outlierCount = nnz(outlierLocations)
%%
%[text] ## 4. Live Task — Find and Remove Trends
%[text] Add **Task \> Find and Remove Trends**. Select `accelerationNoOutliers`, choose a first-order polynomial trend, and name the output `accelerationDetrended`. Use the task plot to explain the difference between the slow sensor drift and the dynamic vibration content.
%[text] The generated-code equivalent is:
accelerationDetrended = detrend(accelerationNoOutliers, 1);
%%
%[text] ## 5. Live Task — Smooth Data
%[text] Add **Task \> Smooth Data**. Select `accelerationDetrended`, choose **Savitzky-Golay**, start with a window length of 31 samples, and name the output `accelerationClean`. Change the window interactively to show the tradeoff between noise reduction and preserving peaks.
%[text] The generated-code equivalent is:
accelerationClean = smoothdata(accelerationDetrended, 'sgolay', 31);
%%
%[text] ## 6. Compare before and after preprocessing
%[text] The same workflow can begin in a Live Task and end as normal MATLAB code. The cleaned result is now ready for visualization, calculations, an app, or export to another engineering team.
tiledlayout(2,1)
nexttile
plot(time_s, accelerationRaw, Color=[0.55 0.55 0.55])
grid on
ylabel('Raw (g)')
title('Before preprocessing')
nexttile
plot(time_s, accelerationClean, Color=[0 0.4470 0.7410], LineWidth=1.1)
grid on
xlabel('Time (s)')
ylabel('Clean (g)')
title('After Live Task workflow')
rawValid = accelerationRaw(~isnan(accelerationRaw));
comparison = table( ...
    [numel(accelerationRaw)-numel(rawValid); nnz(isnan(accelerationClean))], ...
    [outlierCount; 0], ...
    [sqrt(mean(rawValid.^2)); sqrt(mean(accelerationClean.^2))], ...
    RowNames={'Raw measurement','After preprocessing'}, ...
    VariableNames={'MissingSamples','ImpulseOutliers','RMS_g'});
comparison
%%
%[text] ## 7. Optional App — Data Cleaner
%[text] ![](text:image:45e5)
%[text] For users who mainly work with CSV tables, open **Apps \> Data Cleaner** and import `mechanicalData` from the workspace. Show the Data, Summary, and Visualization views; apply one cleaning step; then choose **Export \> Generate Function**. This demonstrates that MATLAB can turn an interactive cleaning history into repeatable code.
openDataCleaner = false;
if openDataCleaner
    dataCleaner
end
%%
%[text] ## 8. Signal Processing App — Signal Analyzer
%[text] ![](text:image:277a)
%[text] Run this section with `openSignalAnalyzer` set to `true`, or open **Apps \> Signal Analyzer**. The timetable carries the time information into the App automatically.
%[text] In Signal Analyzer, demonstrate this customer workflow:
%[text] 1. Plot `RawAcceleration` and `CleanAcceleration` together.
%[text] 2. Add a **Spectrum** view and identify dominant frequency content.
%[text] 3. Select the raw signal, click **Preprocess**, and try Fill Missing, Detrend, Smooth, or Lowpass.
%[text] 4. Open **Measurements** to display signal statistics and peak values.
%[text] 5. Click **Generate Function** to turn the App operations into reusable MATLAB code. \
analysisSignals = timetable(seconds(time_s), accelerationRaw, accelerationClean, ...
    VariableNames={'RawAcceleration','CleanAcceleration'});
openSignalAnalyzer = false;
if openSignalAnalyzer
    signalAnalyzer(analysisSignals)
end
%%
%[text] ## 9. Optional App — Filter Designer
%[text] ![](text:image:233a)
%[text] If the customer wants to understand filter specifications, open **Apps \> Filter Designer** or set the switch below to `true`. Use a sample rate of `2000 Hz`, select a lowpass response, compare FIR and IIR choices, and show magnitude and phase responses. The App can export a filter object or generate MATLAB code.
sampleRate_Hz = 1/median(diff(time_s))
openFilterDesigner = false;
if openFilterDesigner
    filterDesigner
end
%%
%[text] ## 10. Takeaway and future customized application
%[text] The customer can start with Live Tasks and built-in MATLAB apps for immediate productivity. When a repeatable workflow needs to be distributed to other users, the same generated code can be placed behind a focused App Designer interface such as `WDSignalExplorer.mlapp`.
%[text] GUIDE-to-App Designer migration is a later step. It should begin after WD identifies one priority GUIDE application and provides its `.fig`, matching `.m` file, representative CSV inputs, and required user behavior.
appFile = fullfile(workshopFolder, 'WDSignalExplorer.mlapp');
appDesignerDemoAvailable = isfile(appFile)

